#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147288 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7bad79c3-d90e-5752-a3ea-381e9445ab15" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Simple Harmonic Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "54c15528-aec0-5554-b3b0-f928f3a6d3d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6619" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?\n\n- $y'' + 81y = 0$\n\n- $y'' + 9y'+81y = 0$\n\n- $y'' - 81y = 0$" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- Equation I takes the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 81 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\omega = 9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I represents SHM.\n\n- Equation II does not take the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nbecause it has a $y'$ term.\n\n- Equation III does not take the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt would require $\\omega^2 = -81,$ which is not possible for any real number $\\omega$ since $\\omega^2 \\geq 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147289 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef654722-031d-51cc-86fc-a0cbc498b350" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/id #uuid "4c6cb7ad-3d0d-5d85-bda7-a9c8874ce25b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6620" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 100y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere the period $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 100\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 10,\n$$\n\nso the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{10} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\,\\textrm{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147290 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Simple Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "2ca66468-091b-5f69-99eb-2d347486becd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6621" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 9 y = 0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y = R\\cos(3t+\\phi_1)$\n\n- $y = R\\sin(9t+\\phi_2)$\n\n- $y = A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3t}$\n\nNote that $R,\\phi_1,\\phi_2, A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2y = 0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n- $y = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n- $y =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 9\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\omega=3$ into the three different forms of the general solution gives the following:\n\n- $y = R\\cos{(3t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n- $y = R\\sin{(3t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n- $y =A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3 t}$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147291 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Amplitude of an Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/id #uuid "2e1c98d9-bce3-5b6a-bbef-ee32fa2a2448" :question/math-academy-id "e-6622" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 36y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $3\\,\\textrm{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0,\n$$\n\nand the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi)},\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 36\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 6,\n$$\n\nso the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{(6t+\\phi)}.\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0) = 0.$ Substituting this into the equation above gives\n\n$$\nR\\sin\\phi = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\phi = 0.\n$$\n\nSince $\\phi=0,$ our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{6 t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $3\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that $y'(0) = 3.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = 6R\\cos{6t},\n$$\n\nand substituting $y'(0) = 3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n3 = 6R\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad R=\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\textrm{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147292 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"}] :question/id #uuid "7489abc3-36e8-5c4d-a2cf-a2bcb14b63ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-6623" :question/problem "A particle of mass $36\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is $y'' + \\dfrac{1}{2}y = 0$\n\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi}\\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}$\n\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)},$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m=36$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k=9$ has\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = \\dfrac{k}{m} = \\dfrac{9}{36} = \\dfrac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nso its equation of motion is \n\n$$\ny'' + \\dfrac{1}{4}y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich is different from the equation in statement I.\n\n- Statement II is true. The frequency $f$ (measured in $s^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf = \\dfrac{\\omega}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi} \\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\\,R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1}),\\,R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $\\omega = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $\\phi_1 = \\phi$ in the form $R\\sin{(\\omega t+\\phi_2)}$ gives\n\n$$\ny(t) = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7bad79c3-d90e-5752-a3ea-381e9445ab15" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Simple Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8c3b9be-b025-58e7-953a-51cd756d15e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 4y^{′} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146474} {:answer/id #uuid "abd0ed18-0fa2-55e1-b19a-ae8ceaac686b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 4y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146475} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1a3548-b49e-58b0-a316-007a0d95f542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 4y^{2} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146476} {:answer/id #uuid "e7437f43-77d1-51f2-be49-53187f6f5bf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} - 4y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146477} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4d3563-a2a7-5116-ba77-a52069fb7e4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 4y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abd0ed18-0fa2-55e1-b19a-ae8ceaac686b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 4y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146475} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bb5f331-164d-50b0-b86d-c194735fcfdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "916caca2-b090-554c-9711-817f0993a96f" :question/math-academy-id "q-64217" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents SHM is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nIn the above case:\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148079 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef654722-031d-51cc-86fc-a0cbc498b350" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97ddf765-6ff6-5313-af16-a5b29b0329d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{4}s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146486} {:answer/id #uuid "97e23f17-e280-5211-bdc5-666bf373939a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2πs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146487} {:answer/id #uuid "1bdc0645-0107-5fde-9ed0-38b1df5a35a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146488} {:answer/id #uuid "33c126a9-d76e-5c49-bde0-d45c20e08ab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{8}s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146489} {:answer/id #uuid "5d666e96-6aca-5760-a363-950a3b1b2a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{16}s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bdc0645-0107-5fde-9ed0-38b1df5a35a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146488} :answer-field/id #uuid "b14b9e37-a626-5676-8ad5-9f2457873f96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3709cb88-641b-50e9-b4b6-d0a4c2087fba" :question/math-academy-id "q-64221" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThe period $T$ is given by:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\omega }\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6c6414f-229a-5c87-b7db-2c91aef0b4c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} - 4y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146498} {:answer/id #uuid "e724930a-6e42-58c1-b98c-58d392e1be4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 2y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146499} {:answer/id #uuid "e82548b1-ed84-5294-a253-10d6938fbbaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 4y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146500} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a0b027-083e-54cc-af6a-4dfccb1dcb1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + \\sqrt{2}y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146501} {:answer/id #uuid "16eb5462-cee1-5acd-8093-75a706390312" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} - \\sqrt{2}y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e724930a-6e42-58c1-b98c-58d392e1be4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 2y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146499} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f0f923c-6682-5c68-9e0d-dd1c6e325664" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2bb1b50c-fcc3-53d2-91c4-0d8dd7036065" :question/math-academy-id "q-64229" :question/problem "A particle of mass $12\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=24\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{24}{12}=2.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7bad79c3-d90e-5752-a3ea-381e9445ab15" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Simple Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdfce4b8-647e-5342-8bf6-add59939e0a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-9y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347793} {:answer/id #uuid "aa713c1a-a7b6-5e54-bd4f-83a99b256465" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9{y}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347794} {:answer/id #uuid "a270d992-647e-5ee9-bcce-de4677ba6aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9{y}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347795} {:answer/id #uuid "d5bb3e06-1e5c-516e-a606-541dff02272f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347796} {:answer/id #uuid "1c894a6c-2b10-5358-9480-94ee2d9d3f5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+9y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5bb3e06-1e5c-516e-a606-541dff02272f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347796} :answer-field/id #uuid "19c98ad8-be18-5fd1-aab4-eaf5508cc437" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff1986bf-d262-5ccf-8919-3cde9e5e181f" :question/math-academy-id "q-64247" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents SHM is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=0.\n$$\n\nIn the above case:\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef654722-031d-51cc-86fc-a0cbc498b350" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33948804-9e48-5bc6-af8f-096f64aced2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347800} {:answer/id #uuid "9792f5e8-b49a-5d7a-9639-f4e8d2427744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347801} {:answer/id #uuid "1f84a930-159c-5339-8a5c-fe78024c9cbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347802} {:answer/id #uuid "e88ecc89-2f48-5c5b-aa99-9f0cf6a47b4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347803} {:answer/id #uuid "3b62f36e-9be6-5ddc-bca7-b24af9d8799b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e88ecc89-2f48-5c5b-aa99-9f0cf6a47b4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347803} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3f923dc-39b5-5b38-97c5-8bede8037613" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "466a6846-b149-57dc-86b2-1fcf81d1afaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-64250" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=1\\,⟹\\,\\omega =1.\n$$\n\nThe period $T$ is given by:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\omega }\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{1}=2\\pi \\,\\text{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Simple Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c018f2d7-d7c6-587e-8860-51a4962fe485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347807} {:answer/id #uuid "82bc6f20-9b80-568f-89c4-2a91a55f615a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347808} {:answer/id #uuid "300647d7-9ca6-5331-a12f-c7402de2fb3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347809} {:answer/id #uuid "90aff64b-40ad-5d28-9a78-e508958c5a1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347810} {:answer/id #uuid "c15ac956-9a5f-5f52-a0ea-53595b72ad32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82bc6f20-9b80-568f-89c4-2a91a55f615a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347808} :answer-field/id #uuid "4498c0e7-b4fc-5b12-acbd-8efa606ebc8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30ec0217-3ff6-524b-880d-7c9a61fc1c5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-64252" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+81y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=81\\,⟹\\,\\omega =9.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Amplitude of an Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a95dfd5-717e-5439-a97e-19e8ae4e8ae2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347814} {:answer/id #uuid "c650af33-cd36-5197-8a7f-797cfc76480d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347815} {:answer/id #uuid "60acd33a-75c3-58a1-b031-34dff62a081c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347816} {:answer/id #uuid "1925c158-9f84-5270-826c-0ad56fbc5851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347817} {:answer/id #uuid "fe9b034a-f4e4-50ff-a31a-b8115cfd73b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a95dfd5-717e-5439-a97e-19e8ae4e8ae2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347814} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea1b4a19-42fd-5d9c-b6cf-9c3aef296fc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d81de893-b23d-5af6-ac6e-27b0b49baeee" :question/math-academy-id "q-64253" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+64y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=64\\,⟹\\,\\omega =8.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $4\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=4.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8R\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n4=8R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Amplitude of an Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d7fe1e7-05fa-5407-906a-6234e021a8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146540} {:answer/id #uuid "b60c7201-6a6a-53d4-a03f-262c14513316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146541} {:answer/id #uuid "d49102b9-9608-550d-a7ea-f0656947d2a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146542} {:answer/id #uuid "ea9e440f-ac3a-5d3e-921a-352386e6de10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146543} {:answer/id #uuid "3df3185f-2921-5c98-9fe5-09c7c28e3d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b60c7201-6a6a-53d4-a03f-262c14513316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146541} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c1b78e0-b695-5680-93c7-66bdc6619adb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "012482b3-0bd8-57c6-8936-9b9b3da520e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-64254" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+144y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $8\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=144\\,⟹\\,\\omega =12.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡12t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=8.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=12R\\operatorname{cos}⁡12t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n8=12R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡12t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "532f318f-ffb1-56ce-a0c7-d7d7e51f14eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347821} {:answer/id #uuid "1e937a2f-6b2f-5d0e-ba64-cd4bab91487e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-4y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347822} {:answer/id #uuid "79e7f7fd-2ece-5807-8865-a9add8e72f3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{2}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347823} {:answer/id #uuid "0aa56111-5e00-5839-99bf-f8f70c82ed0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347824} {:answer/id #uuid "f5fc45a7-8311-5d8e-aa58-c9605249b0b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5fc45a7-8311-5d8e-aa58-c9605249b0b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347825} :answer-field/id #uuid "544c537f-d29d-559f-97f9-d59051c19179" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b407710d-82b6-5413-b053-42b11d925365" :question/math-academy-id "q-64255" :question/problem "A particle of mass $8\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=32\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{32}{8}=4.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193a2a0c-115e-5019-b79d-76d12bee3379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347828} {:answer/id #uuid "4beab6ab-a866-557a-9b7c-da9f31065be9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347829} {:answer/id #uuid "f68ec0ef-035e-5abd-bf81-720c3c073497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347830} {:answer/id #uuid "9830b004-0491-5d2e-99e5-d688bb3496a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347831} {:answer/id #uuid "f47cdecf-f0ee-5e1b-adaf-413e74927b66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9830b004-0491-5d2e-99e5-d688bb3496a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347831} :answer-field/id #uuid "be5dc12e-937a-5396-b673-5b5d414d4041" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "159cb1ab-7613-5b55-bdef-c9e84b2bbb54" :question/math-academy-id "q-64258" :question/problem "A particle of mass $32\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0$\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{4}+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{4}$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{8}{32}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}$ in form 1 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef654722-031d-51cc-86fc-a0cbc498b350" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "801503cb-58c9-5bc3-9eb3-76d18c9a8d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347835} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5e86d1-b1d1-5eb2-8acd-571c9a80d34b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347836} {:answer/id #uuid "45f5fc86-4513-55db-a74a-52cb560499f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347837} {:answer/id #uuid "0a275af0-a3b9-5e4e-ad0e-6708185fb8fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347838} {:answer/id #uuid "53aae7c4-ce0d-5528-9b21-7f86e54d7044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "801503cb-58c9-5bc3-9eb3-76d18c9a8d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347835} :answer-field/id #uuid "82b29aff-d9e7-5b82-a713-f173b58ba04a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3605b04a-15fd-50ac-9f72-ed54fb63e1dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-79485" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+36y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=36\\,⟹\\,\\omega =6.\n$$\n\nThe period $T$ is given by:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\omega }\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Simple Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe253760-a70f-52a1-b564-9847905ca0a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347842} {:answer/id #uuid "0ba3b544-cdf6-597b-88a6-a39ad5688374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347843} {:answer/id #uuid "f12927cc-8327-5864-9f60-0d40fcd56a7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347844} {:answer/id #uuid "1c30f992-6426-5748-b05a-d18cd90a9c6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347845} {:answer/id #uuid "a60c204b-670b-52ee-bbf9-36c00385a5b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ba3b544-cdf6-597b-88a6-a39ad5688374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347843} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f502ef3-516a-59f2-9e5f-2d7b226570ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66fc565d-c8ed-53ce-9b96-5834b31ff064" :question/math-academy-id "q-79487" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2}t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2}t$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Amplitude of an Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "538663fb-1e95-5331-bcba-362600490501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347849} {:answer/id #uuid "8bb97c6f-fa22-54b4-9207-eaad2a3fdc23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347850} {:answer/id #uuid "b0857941-97d3-533d-adbd-03f501d0585f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347851} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd28336-979b-5ffd-b4eb-ae1b39767cfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347852} {:answer/id #uuid "74e4f416-2900-5f05-8123-58875e5c009e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74e4f416-2900-5f05-8123-58875e5c009e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347853} :answer-field/id #uuid "d17d438e-89ca-5ae1-8f9b-aa4f60df8942" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb0bc318-53b7-59c2-b2a3-48178245e046" :question/math-academy-id "q-79493" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $6\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $6\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=6.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3R\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=6$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n6=3R\\,⟹\\,R=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $2\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7bad79c3-d90e-5752-a3ea-381e9445ab15" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Simple Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3808c3a3-3e47-5303-b308-abb0d857ab67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347856} {:answer/id #uuid "6ddca3ce-316e-509a-86a8-dc9d48b6fca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347857} {:answer/id #uuid "854791ed-943c-53b2-bc61-288f75d8510b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347858} {:answer/id #uuid "d63d5103-50bf-5643-a7c3-2f4a149dffee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347859} {:answer/id #uuid "db22f26a-1035-5517-b6e9-a973d3ef6d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3808c3a3-3e47-5303-b308-abb0d857ab67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347856} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd265e88-c2fb-599b-84ac-2d8631a88138" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bad32adb-c173-579d-bebb-a8366eb9988c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94263" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents SHM is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=0.\n$$\n\nIn the above case:\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7bad79c3-d90e-5752-a3ea-381e9445ab15" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Simple Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e77d9ba1-a35c-51f0-8ecf-1cc6a78b6b72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347863} {:answer/id #uuid "9b08ce8f-a0f9-55c3-bac4-cf6ec855dcfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+8y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347864} {:answer/id #uuid "deebde55-dff1-530e-a908-7b1a97a8378a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347865} {:answer/id #uuid "3252ccc3-a3a7-572f-a67c-48bcab42ebe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347866} {:answer/id #uuid "614f373e-8017-5a5c-95bd-fa461a92fdbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+16{y}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e77d9ba1-a35c-51f0-8ecf-1cc6a78b6b72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347863} :answer-field/id #uuid "52b9b380-ab4d-5ba6-8ccf-d87decc9bed9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "873cc1aa-ff8b-506b-b81a-49f440b9d6fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-94267" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents SHM is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0.\n$$\n\nIn the above case:\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7bad79c3-d90e-5752-a3ea-381e9445ab15" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Simple Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45517759-afc6-51a1-a051-4fedb984fd87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-4y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347870} {:answer/id #uuid "10f4727e-c451-584d-91e5-d5cda4e9a124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347871} {:answer/id #uuid "97de58c6-cad0-5bf5-9f38-92a021d6d728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347872} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9894c9-c90b-528e-97da-090158f81c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+8y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347873} {:answer/id #uuid "b1145be8-bc61-551f-b464-54e0abc66124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9{y}^{′}+3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b9894c9-c90b-528e-97da-090158f81c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+8y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347873} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e61a434-ddb1-538b-8453-3d6b356d5390" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "722a3581-8ce0-5ec8-baf3-aa06bcfd2a36" :question/math-academy-id "q-94268" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents SHM is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8y=0.\n$$\n\nIn the above case:\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=8\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2\\sqrt{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef654722-031d-51cc-86fc-a0cbc498b350" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bec8c917-9844-534c-8a96-92b2a9969447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347877} {:answer/id #uuid "20e3a75b-0a00-52fb-9532-3fb8deabaac1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347878} {:answer/id #uuid "7a5521a5-4974-56db-a3b7-9ace80bcaa0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347879} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc34f81-dda3-5e90-a6a5-b5332082f2f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347880} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d2cc3d-55e4-54e9-9b16-7863ed3aa9bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20e3a75b-0a00-52fb-9532-3fb8deabaac1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347878} :answer-field/id #uuid "774fe256-f408-5dc3-8797-dccc18b57a3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9506b23c-a4ad-56fe-b7ef-0c02bd7aa0ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-94271" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThe period $T$ is given by:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\omega }\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\pi \\,\\text{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef654722-031d-51cc-86fc-a0cbc498b350" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4a577a3-d374-575b-a120-5e1b920c8585" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347884} {:answer/id #uuid "f033f8cb-ad82-5e35-8f7b-b0bb33627470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347885} {:answer/id #uuid "a7094b6d-dc16-5d1b-8370-6d00bdf9d7bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{144}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347886} {:answer/id #uuid "707a35c8-caad-5833-b7de-1096fac4f495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347887} {:answer/id #uuid "0b7fab34-c33e-560f-88bf-0088ac6b3667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "707a35c8-caad-5833-b7de-1096fac4f495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347887} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d147559-0650-5f05-a279-f5621d7d8acb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d55619d9-04e6-5e22-adf5-2ab8249416dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-94274" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+144y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=144\\,⟹\\,\\omega =12.\n$$\n\nThe period $T$ is given by:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\omega }\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Simple Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eac416e-dba2-5471-97fd-174d5f428e5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347891} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c4a7ec-208a-5a75-8e1b-dd31909d3c74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347892} {:answer/id #uuid "91d54d94-55b3-59b4-b9cb-fa4cfedfad05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347893} {:answer/id #uuid "028751f5-e01b-5e60-8837-5eb3bd6543cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347894} {:answer/id #uuid "77473c6c-d74c-55ce-a380-7688429bdf58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "028751f5-e01b-5e60-8837-5eb3bd6543cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347894} :answer-field/id #uuid "03a730bc-ec4f-59ea-89fe-527c35606f96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6b1b519-5719-57bf-b52d-dec5a9c2c7f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-94276" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡16t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡16t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and II could be used to represent $y(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Simple Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c588a69b-21ff-5b13-ba7f-61de617aca1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347898} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4b472b-2119-5f0a-913a-730ef0b559df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347899} {:answer/id #uuid "9f393e14-7d5e-5c31-8560-4d7c01b4c141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347900} {:answer/id #uuid "383f3c1a-af95-566a-a265-662762442ad0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347901} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a5c813-a340-57f6-a141-7f909717a6a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd4b472b-2119-5f0a-913a-730ef0b559df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347899} :answer-field/id #uuid "81074791-c985-5f03-9676-e010448eda45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5104a46-b15f-584e-909c-ecd7bde371f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-94277" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+49y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R{sin}^{2}⁡(7t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{sec}⁡7t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡7t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=49\\,⟹\\,\\omega =7.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡7t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡7t$\n\nTherefore, only function I could be used to represent  $y(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347903 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Simple Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bade982-7efa-54e0-bf4f-7fd8556f41e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347905} {:answer/id #uuid "47171b38-b4e2-53b9-bc7a-23ff02cfed31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347906} {:answer/id #uuid "053a87b2-696a-52e1-b793-fb646d50d388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347907} {:answer/id #uuid "0c12f5e9-90fb-5c47-a7a5-ae27a7ec95b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347908} {:answer/id #uuid "2c1db7b9-c51c-5d9b-a5b7-8359165afc04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c1db7b9-c51c-5d9b-a5b7-8359165afc04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347909} :answer-field/id #uuid "72053b29-dbc5-5281-8acf-bd9ae7fd08a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23f8b8a5-7c24-563a-a40e-d60abf329c96" :question/math-academy-id "q-94278" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\sqrt{5}t+B\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\sqrt{5}t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=5\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{5}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{5}t$\n\nTherefore, only statement II could be used to represent $y(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347910 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Amplitude of an Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "496c7b11-f702-5012-87e0-4579a2b83b0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347912} {:answer/id #uuid "1de0043c-3238-5249-825f-c4e6bfde157b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347913} {:answer/id #uuid "a7219203-08e9-5690-8b5a-045f99fc4ff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347914} {:answer/id #uuid "ed47b792-bfbe-51b2-bc2f-cc4c8c2ab073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347915} {:answer/id #uuid "e84c053d-9bcc-54ee-830f-545ad40a6c7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7219203-08e9-5690-8b5a-045f99fc4ff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347914} :answer-field/id #uuid "39a30bbd-c3ce-590f-a1c9-0336a5525516" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd131a55-d383-5fe1-8032-dc517653bbff" :question/math-academy-id "q-94280" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=25\\,⟹\\,\\omega =5.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5R\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}=5R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Amplitude of an Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57a282d2-9a93-5005-aa30-af7cf6c9da21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347919} {:answer/id #uuid "67ac4f49-fbe1-5613-8190-1c7179046701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347920} {:answer/id #uuid "945c5516-71f9-523a-bc0b-88dc3521e65e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347921} {:answer/id #uuid "b39adf3b-66de-5973-b46b-95f3653ac246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347922} {:answer/id #uuid "12b540b2-1b8d-51f5-8593-c4970b3980e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57a282d2-9a93-5005-aa30-af7cf6c9da21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347919} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba5b2826-7030-5826-895d-00f809968765" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3cf6d9af-b061-5993-b553-d544e56a866d" :question/math-academy-id "q-94283" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=8\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $4\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=4.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2\\sqrt{2}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n4=2\\sqrt{2}R\\,⟹\\,R=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e412fc93-9fe1-5f1d-8399-9d3eb682e3fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347926} {:answer/id #uuid "6be1dbf9-bc53-5c6f-9ddf-e4bc9ba17646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347927} {:answer/id #uuid "015c5e06-90a8-5651-a527-1d484e92abb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347928} {:answer/id #uuid "18b44069-623b-5305-962f-1acaecf4a697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+8y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347929} {:answer/id #uuid "9886a6b3-db66-5c66-8a3a-3a8b025d5f77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e412fc93-9fe1-5f1d-8399-9d3eb682e3fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347926} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f55f56a-ce6d-57ff-99cf-ffdc5fdb0639" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c79644b9-1bd1-5d4c-a08e-a12fa9aff392" :question/math-academy-id "q-94285" :question/problem "A particle of mass $16\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=4\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that:\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{4}{16}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6214187d-fbb8-5d95-bb10-eec3670ddd81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347933} {:answer/id #uuid "03177b05-c611-5c39-b9b3-f9028491d1d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347934} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c0bfcf-9723-5eca-9ea5-e95139a819b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347935} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d9e126-9c48-553c-8a46-6c9fe84ed935" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347936} {:answer/id #uuid "749cfb50-e0b2-5973-b973-f49a432481bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03177b05-c611-5c39-b9b3-f9028491d1d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347934} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0b50ba6-2aec-59c1-9d00-713d93c0c76b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0f7fe2d4-f6a4-50af-961e-e17d97fe8283" :question/math-academy-id "q-94288" :question/problem "A particle of mass $7\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=21\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+3y=0$\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ),$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{21}{7}=3\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =\\sqrt{3}$ and ${ϕ}_{1}=ϕ$ in form 2 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}]}
